15. Theory of Customs Union
Paper: International Economics (ECNCC301) | Unit: III | Topic: Economic integration – theory of Customs Union
Introduction
Economic integration ranges from preferential trading areas to free trade areas, customs unions (common external tariff), common markets, economic unions and complete integration. Jacob Viner's The Customs Union Issue (1950) created the modern theory.
Static effects: trade creation and trade diversion
Trade creation occurs when the union replaces high-cost domestic production with lower-cost imports from a partner. It raises welfare, since resources are used efficiently and consumers buy more at lower prices.
Trade diversion occurs when imports shift from a lower-cost non-member to a higher-cost member because the member enjoys preferential access. It reduces welfare, as the tariff revenue is lost and the world's cheapest source is bypassed.
The net effect depends on which dominates. A customs union is more likely to be beneficial when:
• Member economies are competitive rather than complementary.
• The pre-union tariffs are high.
• The union covers a large share of world trade, and the common external tariff is low.
• Members are geographically close and have similar costs.
Partial equilibrium analysis
In the standard diagram, a small country removes its tariff on the partner's goods. The consumption gain and production gain triangles show trade creation, while the rectangle of lost tariff revenue on diverted imports shows trade diversion.
Dynamic effects
• Economies of scale in a larger market.
• Increased competition reducing monopoly power.
• Investment stimulus and FDI inflow.
• Technological spillovers.
• Terms-of-trade improvement through bargaining power.
• Possible polarisation of industry in richer regions.
Theory of second best
Lipsey and Meade showed that a move toward free trade by a subset of countries need not improve welfare, since it is a second-best change in a distorted world.
Examples
European Union, Mercosur, SACU. Free trade areas include NAFTA/USMCA, SAFTA and ASEAN.
Conclusion
Customs unions are not automatically welfare-improving. The balance of trade creation and diversion, plus dynamic gains, decides the outcome.